# kit:not-a-real-app
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# TRIAL CORPUS — "Lend"  (an app that DOES NOT EXIST)
#
# The directive above keeps this corpus out of any study whose population is
# real apps — binding saturation (gap #8) above all, whose cross-app noun-reuse
# figure would otherwise include an app I invented myself. It is NOT the same as
# `kit:no-ui`: this corpus is full of UI nouns. The axis is existence.
#
# James, claude-code-bot#92 (2026-09-06): "You could also make up some trial
# projects to progress it."
#
# Every other corpus in this directory was reverse-engineered from shipped
# software. This one is written the way the product claims a corpus is written:
# from a user's stated intent, BEFORE any code exists. The brief it traces to is
# docs/trials/lend-brief.md — no repo, no routes, no source tree, and therefore
# no locators available to copy into bindings.json.
#
# THE POINT OF THIS FILE IS THE MEASUREMENT, NOT THE APP. Running kit.js against
# it answers "what does Kit do when pointed forwards", which nothing has asked
# before. See docs/trials/lend-forward-run.md for what it actually did.
#
# Every behaviour here is `source defined` and traces to a line of the brief.
# Nothing is inferred, because inference is the stage that does not exist yet
# (there is no LLM step in the build) — so an `inferred` line here would be me
# hand-writing something and labelling it as a machine's work.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────


# ═══ The daily loop — "open one page and see what is currently out" ═══

behaviour BEH-SHELF-1 "The shelf lists everything currently lent out and who has it"
  source defined docs/trials/lend-brief.md#brief
  actor owner
  when opens page:Shelf
  then sees region:LoanList
  then sees column:Borrower

behaviour BEH-SHELF-2 "An empty shelf says so rather than showing a blank page"
  source defined docs/trials/lend-brief.md#brief
  actor owner
  given shelf:Empty
  when opens page:Shelf
  then sees region:EmptyState

behaviour BEH-SHELF-3 "Overdue loans are visibly marked"
  source defined docs/trials/lend-brief.md#brief
  actor owner
  given loan:Overdue
  when opens page:Shelf
  then sees badge:Overdue


# ═══ Recording a loan — "record a new loan in a few seconds" ═══

behaviour BEH-LEND-1 "A new loan is recorded from the shelf"
  source defined docs/trials/lend-brief.md#brief
  actor owner
  when opens page:Shelf
  when activates button:LendSomething
  then lands on page:NewLoan
  then sees field:ItemName
  then sees field:Borrower

behaviour BEH-LEND-2 "Recording a loan puts it on the shelf"
  source defined docs/trials/lend-brief.md#brief
  actor owner
  when opens page:NewLoan
  when fills form:NewLoan with ?fields
  when activates button:ConfirmLoan
  then lands on page:Shelf
  then sees region:LoanList

behaviour BEH-LEND-3 "The loan form takes an item, a borrower and a due date"
  source defined docs/trials/lend-brief.md#holes
  actor owner
  when opens page:NewLoan
  provides form:NewLoan.fields = ItemName, Borrower, DueDate


# ═══ Returning — "mark it returned and it leaves the list" ═══

behaviour BEH-RETURN-1 "A loan is marked returned from the shelf"
  source defined docs/trials/lend-brief.md#brief
  actor owner
  given loan:Outstanding
  when opens page:Shelf
  when activates button:MarkReturned
  then sees region:LoanList

# ⚠️ NEGATION — one of the twelve shapes docs/pilots/macro-metrics-prose.md
# named as missing. The brief says the item "leaves the list". The notation has
# `then sees`, and no `then does not see`. Written below as the nearest thing
# the grammar permits, which asserts something WEAKER than the brief:
behaviour BEH-RETURN-2 "A returned loan is no longer on the shelf"
  source defined docs/trials/lend-brief.md#brief
  actor owner
  given loan:JustReturned
  when opens page:Shelf
  then sees region:EmptyState


# ═══ The shapes the notation cannot state — recorded, not smuggled in ═══
#
# These four are NOT written as behaviours above, because writing them would
# mean inventing grammar mid-trial. They are the trial's actual finding. Each is
# a real requirement from the brief that a reader would expect the corpus to
# hold, and the corpus cannot hold it:
#
#   ORDERING     "worst offenders at the top" — the brief's own words. There is
#                no `then ... in order of ...`. A generated test can open the
#                page and see the list; it cannot see that the list is sorted.
#
#   TIMING       a loan becomes overdue when its due date passes. `given
#                loan:Overdue` names a fixture STATE, which smuggles the rule
#                into bindings.json where no reviewer will read it as a rule.
#                The transition itself is unstateable.
#
#   CARDINALITY  one row per loan; a borrower holding three items is three rows.
#                Already named as the blocker on macro-metrics' nine preset
#                cards. Still absent, and here it is a domain rule rather than a
#                locator nit.
#
#   NEGATION     see BEH-RETURN-2 above — the only one of the four I could write
#                at all, and only by asserting a different, weaker fact.
